message.reasoning_content:
The user wants me to repair a CODESYS compilation error in the supplied Structured Text function block. The error message is:

"At least one statement is expected"

This is a compile error, not a functional error. The issue is likely that CODESYS expects statements directly after VAR sections, but my code has empty VAR sections or there's some other syntax issue.

Looking at the code structure:

```st
FUNCTION_BLOCK FB
VAR_INPUT
    demand     : INT;
    runHours   : ARRAY[1..5] OF LINT;
    rotated    : BOOL;
END_VAR
VAR_OUTPUT
    leadPump   : INT := 0;
    lagPump    : INT := 0;
    runningCount : INT := 0;
END_VAR
VAR
    prevLeadPump    : INT := 0;
    prevLagPump     : INT := 0;
    prevRunningCount : INT := 0;
    prevClippedDemand : INT := 0;
    wasDemandZero   : BOOL := TRUE;
END_VAR

VAR
    clippedDemand : INT;
    tmp_i         : INT;
    tmp_minHours  : LINT;
    tmp_minPump   : INT;
    tmp_secondMinHours : LINT;
    tmp_secondMinPump  : INT;
    tmp_mainHoursDiff  : LINT;
END_VAR

    // PUB001: Clip demand to 0..5
    IF demand < 0 THEN
        clippedDemand := 0;
    ELSIF demand > 5 THEN
        clippedDemand := 5;
    ELSE
        clippedDemand := demand;
    END_IF;
```

The problem is that I have two VAR sections back-to-back, and the first one has only variables used for output initialization, but the second one declares temporary variables that are used in the implementation. In CODESYS, you should not have multiple VAR sections of the same type (VAR) - you need to consolidate them into one VAR section.


The solution is to merge the two VAR sections into a single VAR block containing all the internal state and temporary variables together. This removes the duplicate VAR declaration that was causing the compiler to expect statements between them. The statements can then follow immediately after the single END_VAR.

